{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "c1",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [5] explicitly states that there are three subtypes of checkpoints: PASSIVE, FULL, and RESTART.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "There are three subtypes of checkpoints that vary in their aggressiveness: PASSIVE, FULL, and RESTART."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c2",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [5] states that all checkpoints initiated by sqlite3_wal_checkpoint() are PASSIVE.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "All checkpoints initiated by sqlite3_wal_checkpoint() and by the automatic checkpoint mechanism are PASSIVE."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c3",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [5] states that all checkpoints initiated by the automatic checkpoint mechanism are PASSIVE.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "All checkpoints initiated by sqlite3_wal_checkpoint() and by the automatic checkpoint mechanism are PASSIVE."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c4",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [5] mentions that FULL and RESTART checkpoints can only be initiated by a call to sqlite3_wal_checkpoint_v2(), but it does not state that calling this function *always* produces one of these types. It implies other options might exist or default behavior might differ if not specified, but more importantly, the text says 'FULL and RESTART checkpoints... can only be initiated by...', which is a necessary condition for those specific types, not a sufficient condition for every call to v2(). However, looking closely at Passage [5], it says 'The default checkpoint style is PASSIVE... All checkpoints initiated by sqlite3_wal_checkpoint() ... are PASSIVE.' It then says 'FULL and RESTART checkpoints ... can only be initiated by a call to sqlite3_wal_checkpoint_v2()'. It does not explicitly say that *every* call to v2 results in FULL or RESTART (one could potentially call v2 with PASSIVE flag). Therefore, the claim that it *always* produces FULL or RESTART is not supported and likely contradicted by the existence of the PASSIVE subtype which is the default, though v2 allows specifying others. Since the text doesn't explicitly forbid v2 from doing PASSIVE, and claims 'always', it is UNSUPPORTED.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c5",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [5] states that a PASSIVE checkpoint 'might not run to completion if there are concurrent readers or writers'. Passage [7] reinforces that a checkpoint cannot reset the WAL if another connection has a read transaction open. The claim says it is guaranteed to complete as long as no *writing* occurs, ignoring the impact of readers. Passage [5] explicitly lists 'concurrent readers' as a reason it might not complete.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "The default checkpoint style is PASSIVE, which does as much work as it can without interfering with other database connections, and which might not run to completion if there are concurrent readers or writers."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c6",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [7] states that a checkpoint is only able to run to completion and reset the WAL file if there are no other database connections using the WAL file.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "A checkpoint is only able to run to completion, and reset the WAL file, if there are no other database connections using the WAL file."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c7",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [7] establishes the condition that *if* a checkpoint runs to completion, it resets the WAL, and that it *can* only do so if no other connections are using it. It does not guarantee that whenever no other connection is using the WAL, a checkpoint *will* automatically run and reset it. A checkpoint must be invoked (either automatically or manually) to perform the reset. Simply having no active connections doesn't trigger a reset unless a checkpoint operation is executed.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c8",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [4] states that the automatic checkpoint mechanism is implemented as a simple wrapper around sqlite3_wal_hook().",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "(The automatic checkpoint mechanism is implemented as a simple wrapper around sqlite3_wal_hook().)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c9",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [3] states that by default, the checkpoint will be run automatically by the same thread that does the COMMIT that pushes the WAL over its size limit.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "By default, the checkpoint will be run automatically by the same thread that does the COMMIT that pushes the WAL over its size limit."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c10",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [2] states that a long-running read transaction can prevent a checkpointer from making progress. Passage [7] explains that if another connection has a read transaction open, the checkpoint cannot run to completion. This directly contradicts the claim that long-running readers never delay the checkpoint.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "Thus a long-running read transaction can prevent a checkpointer from making progress."
        },
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "If another connection has a read transaction open, then the checkpoint cannot reset the WAL file because doing so might delete content out from under the reader."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

